From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 21:29:42 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] misc: fpc1020: serve the trustlet's file listener and report
 the irq level

Taimen's vendor HAL, decompiled, settles two things this driver got
wrong.

The capture sequence waits on the interrupt line's level, not an edge:
fpc_irq_wait() re-reads the sysfs "irq" attribute (a GPIO level in the
vendor driver) until it reads 1, and fpc_tee_capture_image() arms the
sensor, waits for the level, sends a command, and waits for it again.
The latch this driver exposed cannot express that, and the trustlet's
own commands pulse the line, which a latch turns into false touches.
The interrupt is now requested from an "irq" GPIO, poll() reports
EPOLLIN while that GPIO reads high, and the sysfs attribute reports the
level as the vendor one does. The chardev read() and the latch go.

The template database lives in the normal world's filesystem, reached
from inside the trustlet through QSEE's GP file-service listener, 0x7000
-- the only listener the trustlet uses, at three call sites (read, write,
remove). Register it with the shared buffer size qseecomd gives it
(0x7e000) when the trustlet is loaded, send app calls through
qcom_scm_qseecom_app_send_listener(), and hand each request to userspace:
FPC1020_TEE_IOC_LISTENER_RECV waits for one and copies the shared buffer
out, FPC1020_TEE_IOC_LISTENER_SEND copies the answer back and resumes the
blocked call. What a request means -- which path, and whether it is
allowed -- is decided in userspace, where the filesystem policy belongs;
the layout is QSEE's own, as served by qseecomd's libdrmfs.so. A request
nobody answers within 30 s is failed back to the trustlet.

The kernel no longer sends the sensor INIT command. The vendor HAL sends
it, followed by a sleep command, every time it opens the sensor, and the
command protocol is entirely userspace's otherwise. The skip_init
bring-up parameter goes with it.

Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 drivers/misc/Kconfig   |  18 +-
 drivers/misc/fpc1020.c | 711 +++++++++++++++++++----------------------
 2 files changed, 339 insertions(+), 390 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/misc/Kconfig b/drivers/misc/Kconfig
index 89c30b48091c..9f509912c4af 100644
--- a/drivers/misc/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/misc/Kconfig
@@ -662,18 +662,18 @@ source "drivers/misc/amd-sbi/Kconfig"
 source "drivers/misc/rp1/Kconfig"
 
 config FPC1020
-	tristate "FPC1020/FPC1270 fingerprint sensor power/reset/irq driver"
+	tristate "FPC1020/FPC1270 fingerprint sensor behind a QSEE trustlet"
 	depends on OF && GPIOLIB
 	depends on QCOM_QSEECOM
 	help
-	  Say Y here to support the power, reset and interrupt handling for
-	  an FPC1020 or FPC1270 match-on-chip fingerprint sensor wired to a
-	  TrustZone trustlet over SPI. This driver does not talk to the
-	  sensor itself over SPI -- it owns the IO supply, the reset line
-	  and the interrupt only -- but it does provide /dev/fpc_tee, a
-	  chardev that loads the trustlet and carries libfprint's fpctee
-	  driver's commands to it via qcom_scm_qseecom_app_send(), plus a
-	  latched sysfs "irq" attribute mirroring the same interrupt.
+	  Say Y here to support an FPC1020 or FPC1270 match-on-chip
+	  fingerprint sensor whose SPI bus belongs to a TrustZone trustlet,
+	  as on the Google Pixel 2 XL. The driver owns the sensor's supply,
+	  reset and interrupt lines, loads the trustlet, and provides
+	  /dev/fpc_tee: a transport for commands to the trustlet, a poll()able
+	  interrupt level, and the file-service listener the trustlet stores
+	  its template database through. libfprint's fpctee driver speaks the
+	  command protocol on top of it.
 
 	  This driver can also be built as a module. If so, the module
 	  will be called fpc1020.
diff --git a/drivers/misc/fpc1020.c b/drivers/misc/fpc1020.c
index 329558629375..f7412dc54d00 100644
--- a/drivers/misc/fpc1020.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/fpc1020.c
@@ -1,85 +1,39 @@
 // SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
 /*
- * FPC1020/FPC1270 fingerprint sensor -- power, reset, interrupt, and the
- * /dev/fpc_tee chardev that carries commands to the TrustZone trustlet.
+ * FPC1020/FPC1270 fingerprint sensor behind a QSEE trustlet.
  *
- * This driver owns the sensor's IO supply, its reset line, and its
- * interrupt -- and, as of the /dev/fpc_tee addition below, the one thing
- * that can actually reach TrustZone: qcom_scm_qseecom_app_send() is a
- * kernel-exported symbol, not a syscall, so nothing in userspace could ever
- * call it directly no matter what node existed. This driver still sends the
- * SENSOR no commands over SPI -- it cannot, the SPI pads are fenced by a
- * signed devcfg and are not described in this file or touched by it -- but
- * it does now speak the QSEECom protocol to the trustlet that owns that
- * bus, on userspace's behalf. Matches the discipline stated outright in the
- * vendor driver this was modelled on, drivers/misc/fpc1020_platform_tee.c
- * (FPC AB, GPL-2.0): "This driver will NOT send any commands to the sensor
- * it only controls the electrical parts" -- the SENSOR, not the trustlet.
+ * The sensor's SPI bus belongs to TrustZone; the trustlet
+ * (fpctzappfingerprint) is the only thing that talks to the part. What
+ * Linux owns is the electrical side -- the IO supply, the reset line and
+ * the interrupt line -- plus the one path into the trustlet, which is a
+ * kernel-only SCM call. This driver provides exactly those, and /dev/fpc_tee
+ * to reach them from userspace:
  *
- * The interrupt line (FP_INT_N) is active-low in name but the vendor arms
- * it IRQF_TRIGGER_RISING, once, for the device's whole lifetime -- not
- * re-armed per touch -- and taimen's own shipped HAL (decompiled, see the
- * port's bring-up notes) waits on a *latched* status through a sysfs "irq"
- * attribute: reading it returns the latch, not a raw GPIO level, and any
- * write clears the latch so the next edge can set it again. A stateless
- * level mirror does not fit that protocol -- it would report "already
- * true" forever on a line that idles high and the sensor's own HAL would
- * never block. So the latch is the point of this driver, not an incidental
- * detail: it is the one thing a bare GPIO read from outside the kernel
- * cannot reproduce. /dev/fpc_tee's read() exposes the SAME latch to
- * fprintd; the sysfs attribute stays, unmodified, for the vendor HAL and
- * this port's own bring-up tooling (tools/qfp-*) that already reads it.
- *
- * libfprint's fpctee driver (userspace/libfprint-fpctee, upstreamed into
- * pmaports' libfprint fork as drivers/fpctee/) is the reference for
- * everything below the ioctl boundary: the envelope/cmdbuf split, the
- * buffer sizes, and the poll()+read()-one-byte event contract all come
- * from what that code actually does, not from a spec written here first.
- *
- * Two decisions this file makes, both real interface choices rather than
- * defaults:
- *
- * 1. WHO LOADS THE TRUSTLET. qcom_scm_qseecom_app_load()'s own ABI
- *    (TZ_OS_APP_START_ID_PARAM_ID) never transmits the app's name -- only
- *    the assembled image -- so app_get_id() cannot find it again by name
- *    afterwards (confirmed on hardware: it returns -ENOENT even
- *    immediately after a load that itself reported success). The id is
- *    therefore a value that exists exactly once, at load time, and nowhere
- *    else -- userspace has no channel to it that does not go through this
- *    same kernel-only SCM call. So this driver loads cmnlib (the 32-bit
- *    common library QSEE requires resident before any ELFCLASS32 app,
- *    fpctzappfingerprint included) and the trustlet itself, lazily, on the
- *    first /dev/fpc_tee open(), and keeps the returned app_id for the
- *    lifetime of the binding. A second load attempt within the same boot
- *    (module reload, or a second concurrent open racing the first) is
- *    refused by TrustZone's app-manager (QSEOS_APP_START does not restart
- *    an already-running app) -- there is no recovery for that case, by
- *    construction: the id was never discoverable except at the moment of
- *    that first, already-gone, load. open() fails with whatever
- *    qcom_scm_qseecom_app_load() returns rather than pretending to find
- *    the old id.
- *
- * 2. WHO SENDS INIT. Group 0x0a (SENSOR) cmd 0x00 is a one-time hardware
- *    bring-up step (~761 ms, measured tonight) that every real capture
- *    needs and that fpc-tz.c's transport-independent protocol core has no
- *    concept of -- it is specific to taimen's recovered command table, not
- *    part of the yoshino/nile/loire tables fpc-tz.c already knows. Adding
- *    it there would break that file's whole reason for existing (one
- *    protocol core, several trustlet generations as data). It is scoped to
- *    LOAD, not to any individual request -- an ioctl-level "dumb pipe"
- *    would need userspace to somehow know to send it exactly once ever,
- *    which is the identical retention problem as (1) and has no better
- *    answer in userspace than it does here. So this driver sends INIT
- *    itself, once, immediately after a successful app_load(), as the last
- *    step of the same first-open bring-up sequence that decision (1)
- *    already owns. Every ordinary ioctl(FPC1020_TEE_IOC_XFER) after that
- *    is a dumb pipe: this file does not interpret group/cmd/status,
- *    fpc-tz.c does.
+ *  - open() loads cmnlib and the trustlet on first use and registers the
+ *    trustlet's file-service listener. Loading is once per boot: QSEE refuses
+ *    to start an app that is already running and cannot name its id again.
+ *  - FPC1020_TEE_IOC_XFER carries one command buffer to the trustlet and back,
+ *    uninterpreted. The command protocol (sensor init, capture, enrol,
+ *    identify, template database) lives in userspace, as it does in the
+ *    vendor HAL.
+ *  - poll() reports EPOLLIN while the interrupt line is high. The vendor HAL
+ *    waits for the line's *level* (fpc_irq_wait() re-reads the sysfs "irq"
+ *    attribute until it reads "1"), and so does its capture sequence: arm,
+ *    wait for the level, command, wait again. An edge latch does not fit it --
+ *    the commands themselves pulse the line.
+ *  - The trustlet keeps its template database in the normal world's
+ *    filesystem through QSEE's GP file-service listener (id 0x7000): a
+ *    database command is answered by QSEE with a request in the listener's
+ *    shared buffer, which must be served before the command can finish.
+ *    Filesystem policy belongs in userspace, so the request is handed to
+ *    whoever waits in FPC1020_TEE_IOC_LISTENER_RECV and the answer taken back
+ *    through FPC1020_TEE_IOC_LISTENER_SEND, while the XFER that triggered it
+ *    blocks. The request layout is QSEE's, not this driver's; see
+ *    fpc1020_listener_handle().
  *
  * Copyright (C) 2026 Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
  */
 
-#include <linux/atomic.h>
 #include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/device.h>
 #include <linux/dma-mapping.h>
@@ -93,24 +47,21 @@
 #include <linux/mutex.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
+#include <linux/pm_wakeup.h>
 #include <linux/poll.h>
 #include <linux/regulator/consumer.h>
 #include <linux/sizes.h>
 #include <linux/slab.h>
+#include <linux/spinlock.h>
 #include <linux/sysfs.h>
 #include <linux/uaccess.h>
+#include <linux/unaligned.h>
 #include <linux/wait.h>
 
 #include <linux/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.h>
 #include <linux/firmware/qcom/qcom_tzmem.h>
 
-/* hw_reset() timings, from the vendor driver, unchanged: reset ACTIVE
- * (high), a short settle, RESET (low), a longer settle, ACTIVE again.
- * The vendor's own comment applies here too: chip select cannot be
- * driven from a platform driver, so the trustlet still needs to do its
- * own soft reset over SPI after this pulse brings the part out of
- * electrical reset -- this driver only does the electrical part.
- */
+/* hw_reset() timings, from the vendor driver. */
 #define FPC1020_RESET_HIGH1_US_MIN	100
 #define FPC1020_RESET_HIGH1_US_MAX	200
 #define FPC1020_RESET_LOW_US_MIN	5000
@@ -119,126 +70,106 @@
 #define FPC1020_RESET_HIGH2_US_MAX	5100
 
 /*
- * TrustZone wire shape, reproduced from userspace/libfprint-fpctee's
- * fpc-tz.c and bringup/qseecom-app-load/qseecom_app_load.c (both already
- * proven on hardware) -- do not re-derive these numbers.
- *
- * ENVELOPE: fpc_send_mod_cmd_t (tz_api_yoshino.h, sonyxperiadev), exactly
- * two u32 words -- length of the SEPARATE command buffer, and that
- * buffer's physical address -- inside a 64-byte allocation (the vendor's
- * own buffer size for it; the extra bytes are unused padding, kept to
- * match the proven shape rather than trim it to 8). Sent as BOTH req and
- * rsp to qcom_scm_qseecom_app_send(): the TA's top-level dispatch result
- * lands back in the envelope's own word 0, which this driver does not
- * otherwise inspect.
- *
- * CMDBUF: {group, cmd, status, ...}, rewritten in place by the TA -- what
- * fpc-tz.c calls buf[]. Its largest user (the DB/template commands) is
- * FPC_TZ_BUF_WORDS*4 + 256 = 320 bytes; one page is generous headroom for
- * that plus room to grow, matching the size qseecom_app_load.c already
- * proved this works at.
+ * The request the trustlet sees is a two-word envelope -- the command
+ * buffer's length and physical address -- in a 64-byte allocation, sent as
+ * both request and response (QSEECom_send_modified_cmd() in the vendor HAL).
+ * The length is the command buffer's size, and the trustlet reads it: a
+ * short one is accepted and silently does nothing.
  */
 #define FPC1020_TZ_ENV_SIZE		64u
 #define FPC1020_TZ_CMDBUF_SIZE		PAGE_SIZE
 
-#define FPC1020_TZ_GRP_SENSOR		0x0au
-#define FPC1020_TZ_CMD_INIT		0x00u
-
 #define FPC1020_TZ_FW_DIR		"qcom/msm8998/taimen/"
 #define FPC1020_TZ_CMNLIB_NAME		"cmnlib"
 #define FPC1020_TZ_APP_NAME		"fpctzappfingerprint"
 
-/* qseecom@86600000 { reg = <0x86600000 0x2200000>; }, the vendor DT's
- * secapp-region, verified inside mainline's existing tz_mem reservation.
- */
+/* The vendor DT's secapp-region, inside mainline's tz_mem reservation. */
 #define FPC1020_TZ_REGION_ADDR		0x86600000UL
 #define FPC1020_TZ_REGION_SIZE		0x2200000UL
 
-struct fpc1020_image {
-	struct device *dev;
-	void *virt;
-	dma_addr_t phys;
-	size_t mdt_len;
-	size_t total_len;
+/* QSEE's GP file service, and the shared buffer size qseecomd gives it. */
+#define FPC1020_GPFS_LISTENER_ID	0x7000
+#define FPC1020_GPFS_SB_SIZE		0x7e000
+
+/* How long a blocked trustlet call waits for userspace to serve its file. */
+#define FPC1020_LISTENER_TIMEOUT_MS	30000
+
+/* Userspace ABI, mirrored by libfprint's fpctee driver. */
+struct fpc1020_tee_xfer {
+	__u64 buf;
+	__u32 len;
+	__u32 pad;
 };
 
+struct fpc1020_tee_listener {
+	__u32 id;		/* RECV: out. SEND: in, must match. */
+	__s32 status;		/* SEND: 0 served, nonzero failed. */
+	__u64 buf;
+	__u32 len;
+	__u32 timeout_ms;	/* RECV only. */
+};
+
+#define FPC1020_TEE_IOC_XFER		_IOWR('F', 0x01, struct fpc1020_tee_xfer)
+#define FPC1020_TEE_IOC_LISTENER_RECV	_IOWR('F', 0x02, struct fpc1020_tee_listener)
+#define FPC1020_TEE_IOC_LISTENER_SEND	_IOW('F', 0x03, struct fpc1020_tee_listener)
+
 /*
- * BRING-UP ONLY, not a production interface: request_firmware() for
- * FPC1020_TZ_APP_NAME reproducibly fails with -ELOOP on the device this
- * was first tested on, root cause not yet found (the same inode opens
- * fine through every userspace path tried, including a fresh hardlink and
- * /proc/1/root -- see bringup/fpc-tee-chardev-test/ for the diagnostic
- * trail). Since the trustlet was already loaded resident by an earlier
- * probe this boot, this parameter lets bring-up skip straight to that
- * known app id -- INIT is still sent -- so the chardev, GET_FPC_INFO and
- * fprintd can be exercised without waiting on the firmware-loading bug.
- * A production boot has no way to know this id in advance (see decision
- * 1 in this file's header comment) and must go through the real load.
+ * BRING-UP ONLY: the trustlet survives a module reload but its id does not,
+ * and QSEE will not start it twice. Reusing the id from the first load's log
+ * line is the only way to reach it again without a reboot.
  */
 static unsigned int app_id_override;
 module_param_named(app_id, app_id_override, uint, 0444);
-MODULE_PARM_DESC(app_id, "BRING-UP ONLY: skip the trustlet/cmnlib load and use this already-resident app id instead (0 = load normally)");
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(app_id, "BRING-UP ONLY: use this already-resident trustlet id instead of loading (0 = load)");
 
-/*
- * BRING-UP ONLY: isolate INIT's effect on other commands (e.g. GET_FPC_INFO
- * returning a non-zero status where an earlier, INIT-less probe got the
- * manifest) using the SAME chardev transport on both sides of the A/B,
- * rather than comparing against a different implementation. A caller can
- * still send {0x0a,0x00} through the ordinary ioctl at any time -- this
- * only controls whether THIS driver sends it automatically during bring-up.
- */
-static bool skip_init;
-module_param_named(skip_init, skip_init, bool, 0444);
-MODULE_PARM_DESC(skip_init, "BRING-UP ONLY: do not send INIT during bring-up");
+enum fpc1020_listener_state {
+	FPC1020_LISTENER_IDLE,
+	FPC1020_LISTENER_PENDING,	/* request in sb, nobody has taken it */
+	FPC1020_LISTENER_SERVING,	/* copied out, waiting for the answer */
+	FPC1020_LISTENER_DONE,		/* answer in sb */
+};
+
+struct fpc1020_image {
+	void *virt;
+	dma_addr_t phys;
+	size_t mdt_len;
+	size_t total_len;
+};
 
 struct fpc1020 {
 	struct device *dev;
 	struct gpio_desc *reset_gpio;
+	struct gpio_desc *irq_gpio;
 	struct regulator *vdd_io;
 	int irq;
-	/*
-	 * The latch: set by the ISR, cleared by any write to the "irq"
-	 * sysfs attribute OR by a read() of /dev/fpc_tee -- see the file
-	 * header. irq_wq wakes both the sysfs poll(2) path (via
-	 * sysfs_notify(), independent of this waitqueue) and the chardev's
-	 * own poll()/read().
-	 */
-	atomic_t latched;
 	wait_queue_head_t irq_wq;
 
 	struct miscdevice miscdev;
-	/*
-	 * Serializes ALL TrustZone traffic through this device: the
-	 * one-time load+INIT bring-up AND every later ioctl xfer. Taken
-	 * for the whole bring-up sequence in fpc1020_bring_up_locked(), so
-	 * a second opener blocks behind the first rather than racing it
-	 * into a second, refused, app_load().
-	 */
+
+	/* Serializes bring-up and all trustlet traffic. */
 	struct mutex lock;
 	struct qcom_tzmem_pool *tzpool;
 	bool ready;
 	u32 app_id;
-};
 
-/* Userspace's ioctl struct, mirrored byte-for-byte from
- * userspace/libfprint-fpctee/fpctee.c's `struct fpc_tee_xfer` -- keep
- * these two definitions in sync by hand, the same discipline fpc-tz.c
- * already asks of its own callers.
- */
-struct fpc1020_tee_xfer {
-	__u64 buf;
-	__u32 len;
-	__u32 pad;
+	void *sb;
+	dma_addr_t sb_phys;
+	bool listener_registered;
+
+	spinlock_t listener_lock;
+	enum fpc1020_listener_state listener_state;
+	int listener_status;
